      <title>Should Premier League strugglers stick or twist as pressure on managers mounts?  | Andrew Beasley</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/nov/01/michael-carrick-manchester-united-favourite-premier-league-sack-race</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Unusually, all clubs in the top flight have the same manager with whom they began the season – but the first sacking may not be far away&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;No Premier League manager has yet lost his job this season. In only three of the past 10 campaigns was November reached without a club making a managerial change.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The 2026-27 season commencing with 11 managers who were appointed at some point in 2026 has probably contributed to the early season stability. They have to be given a chance to fashion their side as they see fit. But with roughly a quarter of the season played and only one more round of fixtures before an international break, the first sacking may not be far away.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/nov/01/michael-carrick-manchester-united-favourite-premier-league-sack-race"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
