      <title>How can playing for Manchester City fit into Marc Guéhi’s moral universe? | Barney Ronay</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/30/manchester-city-financial-charges-guilty-marc-guehi-premier-league</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Club’s predicament raises interesting questions about principles, both for individuals and the Premier League&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I felt unbelievably ponderous. My head was made of Aztec stone. I realised I didn’t want to be with all these people. Somebody came at me, a huge individual in silver and blue. I fell at his feet and grabbed one shoe. I started untying the lace. He kicked away from me. I got up and walked off. I was exceedingly hungry.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Not my words. Also not the words of the Manchester City centre-back Marc Guéhi, despite what you were thinking there. This is instead a condensed quote from the mind of Gary Harkness, existentially curious college running-back and the narrator of Don DeLillo’s great American football novel End Zone.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/oct/30/manchester-city-financial-charges-guilty-marc-guehi-premier-league"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
